<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercises xml:id="exercises-3-4">
  <title>Exercises for Section 3.4</title>

  <p>In everything below: when <m>x(t) \gt 0</m> the spring is stretched;
  when <m>x(t) \lt 0</m> it is compressed; and <m>x = 0</m> is the
  equilibrium position. Each problem has a lot of overlap with techniques
  from the subsections, so make sure to reference similar examples when
  stuck! There are some hints available, but think about it on your own
  before looking at them.</p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Q1: Underdamped
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-3-4-1">
    <title>Analyzing an Underdamped System</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        A mass-spring-dashpot system has <m>m = 1</m>, <m>c = 2</m>,
        <m>k = 5</m>, with initial conditions <m>x(0) = -1</m> and
        <m>x'(0) = 1</m>. I will do the first part for you and provide
        you with the specific solution to this IVP:
        <me>x(t) = -e^{-t}\cos(2t).</me>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-1a">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Does the mass start stretched or compressed? Which direction
          does it initially move?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          The values <m>x(0)</m> and <m>x'(0)</m> were already given
          in the problem statement above.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>
          The mass starts <alert>compressed</alert> and initially moves
          toward equilibrium.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-1b">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Give a rough sketch of <m>x(t)</m> based on part (a) and what
          you know about the general behavior of underdamped solutions.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          The sketch should show: starts at <m>x = -1</m> (compressed),
          moves toward equilibrium, crosses <m>x = 0</m> infinitely many
          times with oscillations of decaying amplitude, approaching
          <m>x = 0</m> in the long run.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-1c">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all times <m>t \geq 0</m> at which the mass changes direction.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <me>t = \frac{\arctan\!\left(-\frac{1}{2}\right) + n\pi}{2}, \quad n = 1, 2, 3, \ldots</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-1d">
      <statement>
        <p>
          When is the mass most stretched? When is it most compressed?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Direction changes occur at critical points of <m>x(t)</m>.
          For an underdamped system, critical points alternate between
          local maxima (most stretched at that moment) and local minima
          (most compressed at that moment). Because the amplitude is
          always decaying, the overall most stretched position occurs
          at the <em>first</em> local maximum, and the overall most
          compressed position occurs at the <em>first</em> local minimum.
          Use your graph from (b) to determine which comes first (local
          max or local min), then use your answer from part (c): the
          first two values of <m>n</m> correspond to the two answers.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Most compressed at <m>t = 0</m>. Most stretched at
          <m>t = \dfrac{\arctan\!\left(-\frac{1}{2}\right) + \pi}{2}</m>
          (the first critical point, <m>n = 1</m>).
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-1e">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In a sentence or two, describe what happens to the amplitude
          of the oscillations over time.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          The amplitude decays to zero: each successive peak is smaller
          than the last, and <m>\lim_{t \to \infty} x(t) = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Q2: Overdamped: position function given, never crosses
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-3-4-2">
    <title>Analyzing an Overdamped System</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The position function of a mass-spring-dashpot system is
        <me>x(t) = -4e^{-t} - e^{-5t}.</me>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-2a">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute <m>x(0)</m> and <m>x'(0)</m>. What do these tell you
          about where the mass starts and which direction it initially moves?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>x(0) = -5</m>: starts <alert>compressed</alert>.
          <m>x'(0) = 9</m>: initially moves toward equilibrium.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-2b">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Does the mass ever pass through the equilibrium position <m>x = 0</m>?
          Justify mathematically.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Solve <m>x(t) = 0</m> and think carefully about the signs of
          each term.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <alert>No.</alert> Both terms of <m>x(t)</m> are always negative,
          so <m>x(t) = 0</m> has no solution.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-2c">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Give a rough sketch of <m>x(t)</m> based on your answers in
          (a) and (b).
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          The sketch should show: starts at <m>x = -5</m> (compressed),
          moves toward equilibrium, never crosses <m>x = 0</m>, and
          approaches zero from below as <m>t \to \infty</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-2d">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is the spring ever stretched? Explain.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Look at your graph from (c). Consider what you know about
          <m>x(0)</m> and whether the mass ever crosses equilibrium.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <alert>No.</alert> Since <m>x(0) \lt 0</m> and the mass never
          crosses equilibrium, <m>x(t) \lt 0</m> for all <m>t \geq 0</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-2e">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe the full motion of the mass from <m>t = 0</m>
          to infinity in words.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          The mass starts compressed at <m>x = -5</m>, moves toward
          equilibrium, never crosses, and slowly approaches <m>x = 0</m>
          from below.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Q3: Overdamped: solve IVP, crosses equilibrium
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-3-4-3">
    <title>Analyzing an Overdamped System: Finding Maximum Stretch and Compression</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        A mass-spring-dashpot system has <m>m = 1</m>, <m>c = 5</m>,
        <m>k = 6</m>, with initial conditions <m>x(0) = -2</m> and
        <m>x'(0) = 7</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-3a">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the position function <m>x(t)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <me>x(t) = e^{-2t} - 3e^{-3t}.</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-3b">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Does the mass ever pass through equilibrium <m>x = 0</m>?
          Justify mathematically.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <alert>Yes</alert>, at <m>t = \ln 3</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-3c">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Give a rough sketch of <m>x(t)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          The sketch should show: starts at <m>x = -2</m> (compressed),
          moves toward equilibrium, crosses <m>x = 0</m> at
          <m>t = \ln 3</m>, reaches a maximum on the stretched side,
          then slowly approaches zero from above.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-3d">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the time of maximum stretch and maximum compression, or
          determine that the spring is never stretched or never compressed.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          First use your graph and the equilibrium crossing from (b) to
          determine whether the spring is ever stretched and/or ever
          compressed. Then recall that maxima and minima occur either at
          <m>t = 0</m> or at a critical point where <m>x'(t) = 0</m>. Use your graph to determine which case applies for each.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Most compressed at <m>t = 0</m>. Most stretched at
          <m>t = \ln\!\tfrac{9}{2}</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-3e">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What does the mass do after crossing the equilibrium position?
          Describe in words.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Recall the long-term behavior of overdamped solutions. How does
          this connect to what happens after the crossing? Also consider:
          which side of equilibrium is the mass on after it crosses?
        </p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>
          After crossing, the mass is on the stretched side. It reaches
          maximum stretch at <m>t = \ln(9/2)</m>, then stays on the
          stretched side and slowly approaches <m>x = 0</m> from above.
          It never crosses equilibrium again.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Q4: Critically damped: solve IVP, starts compressed
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-3-4-4">
    <title>Analyzing a Critically Damped System</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        A mass-spring-dashpot system has <m>m = 1</m>, <m>c = 4</m>,
        <m>k = 4</m>, with initial conditions <m>x(0) = -1</m> and
        <m>x'(0) = -3</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-4a">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the position function <m>x(t)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <me>x(t) = -e^{-2t}(1 + 5t).</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-4b">
      <statement>
        <p>
          When is the spring most compressed? Is the spring ever stretched?
          Describe the full motion of the mass after the point of maximum
          compression.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Work through the following steps:
          <ul>
            <li>Use <m>x(0)</m> and <m>x'(0)</m> to determine where the
            mass starts and which direction it initially moves.</li>
            <li>Solve <m>x(t) = 0</m> to check whether the mass ever
            crosses equilibrium <m>x = 0</m>.</li>
            <li>Use these facts to draw a rough sketch of <m>x(t)</m>.</li>
            <li>From the sketch, you can determine whether the spring is
            ever stretched or compressed. Then decide: does the
            maximum compression occur at <m>t = 0</m> or at a critical
            point where <m>x'(t) = 0</m>?</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>
          The only solution to <m>x(t) = 0</m> is <m>t = -\tfrac{1}{5} \lt 0</m>,
          so the mass never crosses equilibrium and is never stretched.
          Most compressed at <m>t = \tfrac{3}{10}</m>.
          After that, the mass moves back toward equilibrium, staying
          on the compressed side and approaching <m>x = 0</m> from below.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Q5: Critically damped harder: position function given
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-3-4-5">
    <title>Analyzing a Critically Damped System: Finding Maximum Stretch</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The position function of a mass-spring-dashpot system is
        <me>x(t) = e^{-2t}(2t - 4).</me>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-5a">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>x(0)</m> and <m>x'(0)</m>, determine whether the mass
          crosses the equilibrium position <m>x = 0</m>, and use this
          information to give a rough sketch of <m>x(t)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Follow the same steps as in the previous questions:
          (1) find <m>x(0)</m> and <m>x'(0)</m> and interpret them,
          (2) solve <m>x(t) = 0</m> to check for equilibrium crossings,
          (3) use these facts to sketch the graph.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>x(0) = -4</m> (compressed), <m>x'(0) = 10</m> (toward equilibrium),
          crosses equilibrium at <m>t = 2</m>. Sketch: starts at <m>x = -4</m>,
          crosses at <m>t = 2</m>, reaches a maximum on the stretched side,
          then approaches zero from above.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-5b">
      <statement>
        <p>
          At what time <m>t^*</m> is the mass farthest from equilibrium,
          and what is the position <m>x(t^*)</m> at that time?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          The farthest point from equilibrium occurs either at <m>t = 0</m>
          or at a critical point where <m>x'(t) = 0</m>. Use your sketch
          from part (a) to determine which case applies, then solve
          <m>x'(t) = 0</m> to find <m>t^*</m>. Finally, plug <m>t^*</m>
          back into <m>x(t)</m> to find the actual position.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>t^* = \tfrac{5}{2}</m>, and <m>x\!\left(\tfrac{5}{2}\right) = e^{-5}</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ex-3-4-5c">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe the motion of the mass after it reaches maximum stretch.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          The mass stays on the stretched side, never crossing equilibrium
          again, and slowly approaches <m>x = 0</m> from above.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

  </exercise>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-3-4-ex" iframe="question-form.html?section=3.4 exercises" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>

</exercises>
